From b9c2bd25b7c5fe97b0fdc2745d56f85fd5d036d5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Matthew Jackson <matthew@pq.io>
Date: Sun, 19 Jul 2026 09:08:07 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 2/4] hw/display: add apple-gfx-pci device for Linux hosts

Add a Linux-host implementation of the apple-gfx-pci paravirtualized
graphics device. It exposes the same guest-visible PCI device as the
macOS-host apple-gfx-pci.m (vendor 0x106b, device 0xeeee), so an
unmodified macOS guest binds its stock AppleParavirtGPU.kext driver,
but the host side is plain C backed by the external libapplegfx-vulkan
library instead of Apple's ParavirtualizedGraphics.framework.

The split mirrors the macOS implementation: apple-gfx-common-linux.c
carries the transport-agnostic core (task and memory management via
the library's shell callbacks, interrupt injection through legacy MSI,
display frame/cursor callbacks bridged onto QEMU's console layer via
bottom halves, MMIO forwarding), and apple-gfx-pci-linux.c is the thin
PCI transport (BAR + MSI + QOM boilerplate and properties).

Guest protocol handling, command decoding and rendering live entirely
in libapplegfx-vulkan; rendering works on any Vulkan implementation
including Mesa's software lavapipe driver, so no host GPU is required.

Properties:
 - display-modes: array of <width>x<height>@<refresh> mode strings
 - gpu_cores: caps the library's worker thread pool (0 = auto)

The device is only built when the libapplegfx-vulkan dependency was
found (new 'libapplegfx' meson option); it compiles to nothing on
other configurations, and no existing file's behaviour changes.

Live migration is blocked for now via a migration blocker registered
at realize; the device has no vmstate yet.

Signed-off-by: Matthew Jackson <matthew@pq.io>
